    <h1>How every number is calculated</h1>
    <p class="hero__sub">
      The whole point of this project is that you can check the arithmetic. Here it is, formula by formula, each
      with its source and a link to the code that runs it. Nothing here is a secret sauce; it is all either an
      agency's published rule or a line of algebra.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- 1. The two totals -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>The two totals</h2>
    <p>
      Every other calculator shows you one number. This shows two, and the gap between them is the reason the
      project exists. The <strong>lender total</strong> is what underwriting counts; the <strong>true total</strong>
      adds the one thing a lender does not, the maintenance you will actually spend.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>lender</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>P&amp;I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>MI</mi><mo>+</mo>
        <mi>HOA</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>lender</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>P&amp;I</dt><dd>principal and interest (below)</dd>
      <dt>T, I</dt><dd>property tax, homeowners insurance</dd>
      <dt>MI</dt><dd>mortgage insurance (PMI, FHA MIP, or none)</dd>
      <dt>HOA, M</dt><dd>HOA dues, Mello-Roos / special assessments</dd>
      <dt>R</dt><dd>maintenance reserve, 1% of value a year by default</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      A lender never counts maintenance, so it must never inflate a debt-to-income ratio.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/mortgage.ts">mortgage.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Monthly payment -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>The mortgage payment</h2>
    <p>The standard amortized-loan formula: the fixed monthly payment that retires a loan of <math><mi>P</mi></math> over <math><mi>n</mi></math> months at monthly rate <math><mi>r</mi></math>.</p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>P</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>P</dt><dd>the loan amount, including any financed FHA upfront premium or VA funding fee</dd>
      <dt>r</dt><dd>the annual rate divided by 12</dd>
      <dt>n</dt><dd>the term in months, so 360 for a 30-year loan</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/amortization.ts">amortization.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Balance and LTV over time -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>The balance, and when mortgage insurance ends</h2>
    <p>
      After <math><mi>k</mi></math> payments the remaining balance is the loan grown at the rate, less the payments
      made, also grown. Loan-to-value is that balance over the original purchase price.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup>
        <mo>−</mo>
        <mi>M</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
          <mi>r</mi>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      Conventional PMI has two automatic end points under the Homeowners Protection Act, and whichever comes first
      governs: the month LTV reaches 78% <em>on the original schedule</em> (appreciation does not count), or the
      midpoint of the loan term. FHA is different: put less than 10% down and its premium runs for the
      <strong>life of the loan</strong>, with no equity exit at all.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      Homeowners Protection Act of 1998; HUD Mortgagee Letter 2023-05 for FHA.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/mortgage.ts">mortgage.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. Property tax -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>Property tax, under Proposition 13</h2>
    <p>
      Buying resets the assessed value to your purchase price. The tax is that value, less the homeowners'
      exemption if you file for it, times the county rate.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mo>(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo>
        <mo>·</mo><mi>t</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>V</dt><dd>the purchase price (Prop 13 reassesses to it)</dd>
      <dt>E</dt><dd>the $7,000 homeowners' exemption, if claimed</dd>
      <dt>t</dt><dd>the county rate: the 1% statutory base plus voter-approved bonds</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Mello-Roos is modeled as its own line, never folded into <math><mi>t</mi></math>, because doing so would
      double-count and hide the single most invisible cost in California new construction.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      California Constitution Article XIII A. County rates are hand-verified where we have them, and flagged as a
      statewide fallback where we do not.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/ca-property.ts">ca-property.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. VA residual income -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>VA residual income</h2>
    <p>
      VA does not underwrite on debt-to-income; it underwrites on what is left over each month after everything.
      That residual has to clear a regional minimum, and California is in the West region, which has the highest
      minimums in the country.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>Residual</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>mo</mtext></msub>
        <mo>−</mo><mi>Tax</mi>
        <mo>−</mo><mi>Housing</mi>
        <mo>−</mo><mi>Debts</mi>
        <mo>−</mo><mn>0.14</mn><mo>·</mo><mi>ft²</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>Y<sub>mo</sub></dt><dd>gross monthly income across all earners</dd>
      <dt>Tax</dt><dd>estimated income-tax withholding</dd>
      <dt>0.14 · ft²</dt><dd>VA's fixed utility allowance, 14¢ per square foot per month</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      This is why a VA borrower can be approved at a debt-to-income ratio that would sink a conventional loan, and
      why the 41% figure you may have heard is a guideline, not a cap.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/mortgage.ts">mortgage.ts</a>,
      VA Lender's Handbook M26-7 ch. 4.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Rent versus buy -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>Rent versus buy: two net-worth paths</h2>
    <p>
      The honest version of "rent is throwing money away". Each year, the <strong>buyer's</strong> net worth is
      what the house would fetch after selling costs, less the loan balance:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>buy</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo>
        <mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      The <strong>renter</strong> invests the money the buyer sank into the house, and every month adds or
      withdraws the difference in the two paths' cash costs, compounding at the investment return:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>rent</mtext></msub><mo>←</mo>
        <msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>rent</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>inv</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo>
        <mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>own</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>rent</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      Owning does not always <em>stay</em> ahead. When the investment return you give up is larger than the
      appreciation you buy, the renter's invested capital eventually out-compounds the house: owning leads in a
      middle window, then renting reclaims the lead. So the panel reports whether owning is ahead
      <em>at your hold year</em>, not merely whether it ever crossed.
    </p>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>V, B</dt><dd>the home value and loan balance that year</dd>
      <dt>s</dt><dd>selling costs, 6% by default</dd>
      <dt>c<sub>own</sub>, c<sub>rent</sub></dt><dd>the monthly cost of owning (net of the tax deduction) and of renting</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      Every cost that enters this comes from one <code>bridgeScenario()</code> call, so the itemised comparison and
      every price sweep cannot drift apart.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/rent-vs-buy.ts">rent-vs-buy.ts</a>,
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/scenario-bridge.ts">scenario-bridge.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. The mortgage interest deduction -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>The mortgage interest deduction</h2>
    <p>
      Only the interest above the standard deduction is worth anything, and only on the first $750,000 of debt.
      The relief in a year is:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>Relief</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>τ</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mi>max</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo>
        <mi>min</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mtext>ded</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo>
        <msub><mi>i</mi><mtext>ded</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>τ</dt><dd>combined marginal tax rate</dd>
      <dt>i<sub>ded</sub></dt><dd>deductible interest, capped at the share of the loan under $750,000</dd>
      <dt>O</dt><dd>other itemized deductions</dd>
      <dt>S</dt><dd>the standard deduction for your filing status</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      Internal Revenue Code §163(h); the 2026 standard deduction and SALT cap.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/federal-tax.ts">federal-tax.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Affordability -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>What can you actually afford?</h2>
    <p>
      Run backwards, this is not a formula but a search. Price feeds back into property tax, insurance, the PMI
      band, and the jumbo and FHA limits, so there is no closed form. The engine binary-searches for the highest
      price that still clears every gate: the debt-to-income ceiling, VA residual income, and the FHA county limit,
      which is a hard stop that is not the conforming limit.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      An answer above the conforming limit is quoted at the conforming rate, so it is an upper bound, and the page
      says so on the number.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/affordability.ts">affordability.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Buying power -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>Buying power: the payment formula, inverted</h2>
    <p>
      To ask "what could a median income buy", invert the payment. Given a monthly budget of 30% of income at that
      year's rate, the affordable price is:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>Price</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mn>1</mn>
          <mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow>
        </mfrac>
        <mo>·</mo>
        <mi>M</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
          <mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>M</dt><dd>the monthly budget: 30% of gross income, divided by 12</dd>
      <dt>d</dt><dd>the assumed down payment, 20%</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Holding the <em>effort</em> constant at 30% and letting the price float is what isolates buying power from
      everything else that moved.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/buying-power.ts">buying-power.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 10. The deposit race -->
  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>Saving the deposit, with rent rising</h2>
    <p>
      A renter's saving capacity is not fixed: rising rent eats into it. If non-rent spending tracks wages, next
      year's monthly saving is:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>s</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>)</mo>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <mi>rent</mi><mo>·</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>rent</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>g</dt><dd>nominal wage growth, 3.51% from the California income series</dd>
      <dt>g<sub>rent</sub></dt><dd>rent growth, which you set</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      When rent grows faster than wages the second term is negative and the saving rate falls, which is why a
      renter whose rent outruns wages closes the gap more slowly. The house price, meanwhile, compounds at its
      own appreciation rate, so the deposit is a genuine race.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/rent-vs-buy.ts">rent-vs-buy.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="formula-block">
    <h2>Everything else</h2>
    <p>
      The FHA MIP schedule, the VA funding fee, the PMI rate bands, the FAIR Plan wildfire figures, the AB 1482
      rent caps, the county price histories and the affordability search all follow the same discipline: a
      published rule where one exists, an honest estimate flagged as one where it does not, and a citation on
      every number. The whole engine is a few thousand lines of TypeScript with no dependencies, and it is
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}">open source</a> so you can read all of it.
    </p>
    <p class="method-page__back"><a href="/">← Back to the calculator</a></p>
  </section>
